<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qansuh al-Ghawri</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1441–<a href="/years/1516">1516</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Al-Ashraf Qansuh al-Ghawri</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Qansuh al-Ghawri was the penultimate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, reigning from 1501 to 1516. He built a fleet to fight the Portuguese in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>, which was defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-diu-1509">Battle of Diu</a> in 1509, and left a large mosque and mausoleum complex in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>. He was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-marj-dabiq">Battle of Marj Dabiq</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1516, which opened Syria and then Egypt to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-i">Selim I</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qansuh-al-ghawri.jpg" alt="Qansuh al-Ghawri" width="320" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Florentine painter Cristofano dell'Altissimo, 16th century · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mamluk_Sultan_Al-Ashraf_Qansuh_al-Ghuri_by_Florentine_painter_Cristofano_dell%27Altissimo_Galleria_degli_Uffizi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
